# Practical: Develop Application to Download Image/Video from Server using MTOM Technique

---

# Step 1: Create a New ASP.NET Web Application

1. Open **Visual Studio 2022**.
2. Click **Create a new project**.
3. Search and select **ASP.NET Web Application (.NET Framework)**.
4. Click **Next**.
5. Configure the project:

   * **Project Name:** SimpleCCWebApplication
   * **Framework:** .NET Framework 4.7.2
6. Click **Create**.
7. In the template selection window, choose **Empty**.
8. Click **Create**.

---

# Step 2: Create an Images Folder and Add Content

1. In **Solution Explorer**, right-click the project **SimpleCCWebApplication**.
2. Select **Add → New Folder**.
3. Name the folder **images**.
4. Add image files such as:

```
Image1.gif
download.jpeg
```

These files will be available for download from the server.

---

# Step 3: Create the Web Service (SOAP)

1. Right-click the project.
2. Select **Add → New Item**.
3. Choose **Web Service (ASMX)**.
4. Name it:
DownloadImageWS.asmx
5. Click **Add**.

---

# Step 4: Implement Web Service Logic

Open **DownloadImageWS.asmx.cs** and add the following code.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Web.Services;

[WebService(Namespace = "http://tempuri.org/")]
public class DownloadImageWS : WebService
{

    [WebMethod]
    public byte[] DownloadImage(string imageName)
    {
        string path = Server.MapPath("~/images/" + imageName);

        if (File.Exists(path))
        {
            return File.ReadAllBytes(path);
        }

        return null;
    }

}
```

This method reads the image from the **images folder** and sends it to the client as **binary data**.

---

# Step 5: Enable MTOM in Web.config

Open **Web.config** and ensure the following configuration is added:

```xml
<configuration>
<system.web>
<compilation debug="true" targetFramework="4.7.2" />
</system.web>
</configuration>
```

MTOM allows **efficient transfer of binary data (images/videos)** in SOAP messages.

---

# Step 6: Create a Generic Handler

1. Right-click the project.
2. Select **Add → New Item**.
3. Choose **Generic Handler**.
4. Name it:
MyHandler.ashx
5. Click **Add**.

---

# Step 7: Implement Handler Logic

Open **MyHandler.ashx.cs** and write:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Web;

public class MyHandler : IHttpHandler
{

    public void ProcessRequest(HttpContext context)
    {
        string img = context.Request.QueryString["img"];

        string path = context.Server.MapPath("~/images/" + img);

        context.Response.ContentType = "image/jpeg";
        context.Response.WriteFile(path);
    }

    public bool IsReusable
    {
        get { return false; }
    }

}
```

This handler reads the image from the **server folder** and streams it to the client.

---

# Step 8: Create Web Form Client

1. Right-click the project.
2. Select **Add → Web Form**.
3. Name the file:

```
Downloadimage.aspx
```

---

# Step 9: Design the Web Page

Open **Downloadimage.aspx** and add the following controls.

```html
Enter Image Name:
<asp:TextBox ID="TextBox1" runat="server"></asp:TextBox>

<br/><br/>

<asp:Button ID="Button1" runat="server" 
Text="Download Image and Show" 
OnClick="Button1_Click" />

<br/><br/>

<asp:Label ID="Label1" runat="server"></asp:Label>

<br/><br/>

<asp:Image ID="Image1" runat="server" Height="200px" Width="200px"/>
```

---

# Step 10: Implement Button Click Logic

Open **Downloadimage.aspx.cs** and write:

```csharp
protected void Button1_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{

    string imgname = TextBox1.Text;

    Label1.Text = "Downloading of Image is done";

    Image1.ImageUrl = "MyHandler.ashx?img=" + imgname;

}
```

This will **call the handler and display the image on the page**.

---

# Step 11: Run and Test the Application

1. Right-click **Downloadimage.aspx**.
2. Select **View in Browser**.
3. Enter an image name such as:

```
Image1.gif
```

4. Click **Download Image and Show**.

---

# Step 12: Verify the Result

The browser will display:

```
Downloading of Image is done
```

and the selected **image will appear on the webpage**.

